            <div xml:id="d80">

                <pb facs="0124" n="112" sameAs="frus:frus1943v02/pg112"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.01/2105: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Algiers</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wiley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-05-08T21:00:00-04:00">May 8, 1943—9
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>884. For Murphy. Your no. 805, May 6, 5 p.m. In the military and other
                    circumstances of the general situation the action of General Giraud seems to be
                    free from any objection.</p>
                <p>As he knows, our one primary consideration and concern in the African campaign is
                    the waging of battle until the continent is conquered, and we see no reason,
                    therefore, why political or other considerations should be allowed to interfere
                    with the military effort now proceeding. We have so expressed ourselves to the
                    War Department this morning.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
